      "title": "Mathematical Foundation: Strong Form, Weak Form, and Galerkin Method",
      "children": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "inline_content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The weak formulation transforms a partial differential equation into an integral form by multiplying with a test function and integrating over the domain. Basis functions are selected to approximate the solution within each element, often using polynomials. These basis functions must satisfy continuity requirements across element boundaries. The weak form ensures compatibility with numerical integration and allows for the use of variational principles. In the weak formulation, the solution "
            },
            {
              "type": "inline_math",
              "expression": "u"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " is approximated using basis functions "
            },
            {
              "type": "inline_math",
              "expression": "\\phi_i"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " as "
            },
            {
              "type": "inline_math",
              "expression": "u \\approx \\sum_i u_i \\phi_i"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": ", where "
            },
            {
              "type": "inline_math",
              "expression": "u_i"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " are the unknown coefficients. The basis functions "
            },
            {
              "type": "inline_math",
              "expression": "\\phi_i"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " are typically chosen to be polynomials, such as linear or quadratic functions, to ensure a smooth approximation of the solution within each element. The choice of basis functions is critical in ensuring the accuracy and stability of the numerical solution.\n\nThe coercivity of a bilinear form is a critical property that ensures the existence and uniqueness of the weak solution in finite element analysis. This property is guaranteed by the inequality "
            },
            {
              "type": "inline_math",
              "expression": "a(v, v) \\geq \\alpha \\|v\\|^2"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " "
            },
            {
              "type": "citation_occurrence",
              "occurrence_id": "a1783532-be0c-5b85-898b-20b6ce5192ed",
              "source_id": "wiki_c_a_s_lemma"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": ", which enforces the coercivity of the bilinear form "
            },
            {
              "type": "inline_math",
              "expression": "a"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": ". The coercivity condition, "
            },
            {
              "type": "inline_math",
              "expression": "|B(u, u)| \\geq c\\|u\\|^2 \\quad \\text{for some } c > 0"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " "
            },
            {
              "type": "citation_occurrence",
              "occurrence_id": "ab5836a2-a3d8-5b49-bd67-bd01288bf831",
              "source_id": "wiki_babu_ka_lax_milgram_theorem"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": ", ensures the uniqueness and stability of the weak solution, critical for numerical methods. The Babuška–Lax–Milgram theorem "
            },
            {
              "type": "citation_occurrence",
              "occurrence_id": "c4defeaf-8b38-582f-9cfe-1c4efeb140e3",
              "source_id": "wiki_babu_ka_lax_milgram_theorem"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " provides a framework for proving the existence and uniqueness of the weak solution, relying on the coercivity of the bilinear form.\n\nThe variational equation, "
            },
            {
              "type": "inline_math",
              "expression": "a(u, v) = L(v)"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": ", is a fundamental component of the weak formulation, representing the weak form of the partial differential equation. This equation is derived by multiplying the strong form with a test function and integrating over the domain, as described by the Babuška-Lax-Milgram theorem "
            },
            {
              "type": "citation_occurrence",
              "occurrence_id": "b8fbe07e-07b3-506a-b547-f9f40ba8817a",
              "source_id": "wiki_babu_ka_lax_milgram_theorem"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": ". The variational equation is essential for the finite element method, as it enables the use of variational principles and ensures compatibility with numerical discretization. The coercivity of the bilinear form, "
            },
            {
              "type": "inline_math",
              "expression": "a(v, v) \\geq \\alpha \\|v\\|^2"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " "
            },
            {
              "type": "citation_occurrence",
              "occurrence_id": "7bb84eec-be89-5130-983c-b2dadc37a2b9",
              "source_id": "wiki_c_a_s_lemma"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": ", guarantees the existence and uniqueness of the weak solution, ensuring that the finite element solution converges to the true solution under appropriate conditions."
            }
          ]
        }
