              "source_id": "wiki_c_a_s_lemma"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": ".\n\nThe convergence of the finite element procedure is contingent upon the satisfaction of specific conditions, primarily the coercivity and continuity of the bilinear form. The coercivity condition, "
            },
            {
              "type": "inline_math",
              "expression": "|B(u, u)| \\geq c\\|u\\|^2 \\quad \\text{for some } c > 0"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": ", ensures the uniqueness and stability of the weak solution, while the continuity condition, "
            },
            {
              "type": "inline_math",
              "expression": "|a(v, w)| \\leq \\gamma \\|v\\| \\|w\\|"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": ", prevents the bilinear form from growing unbounded with respect to the input functions. These conditions are crucial for the stability of numerical methods and are a key component of the Babuška–Lax–Milgram theorem, as stated in "
            },
