    {
      "type": "section",
      "section_id": "0557ce05-c337-4bc8-9d53-8f4f1a870fe6",
      "title": "Introduction and Scope of the Finite Element Method",
      "children": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "inline_content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The finite element method (FEM) is a numerical technique used to solve partial differential equations (PDEs) by discretizing the domain into smaller elements. The FEM relies on the weak formulation of the PDE, which transforms the equation into an integral form by multiplying with a test function and integrating over the domain. This approach is central to the FEM as it enables the use of variational principles and ensures compatibility with numerical discretization. The weak form is typically expressed as a bilinear equation involving a bilinear form "
            },
            {
              "type": "inline_math",
              "expression": "a(u, v) = L(v)"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": ", where "
            },
            {
              "type": "inline_math",
              "expression": "u"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " is the solution in the Hilbert space "
            },
            {
              "type": "inline_math",
              "expression": "V"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": ", "
            },
            {
              "type": "inline_math",
              "expression": "v"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " is a test function, and "
            },
            {
              "type": "inline_math",
              "expression": "L(v)"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " is a bounded linear functional. The coercivity of the bilinear form, "
            },
            {
              "type": "inline_math",
              "expression": "a(v, v) \\geq \\alpha \\|v\\|^2"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " "
            },
            {
              "type": "citation_occurrence",
              "occurrence_id": "b028a9e3-d7d5-5e08-baf1-3b0435710cce",
              "source_id": "wiki_c_a_s_lemma"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": ", is a critical property that ensures the existence and uniqueness of the weak solution.\n\nThe weak formulation of the finite element method is a crucial concept that transforms a partial differential equation into an integral form by multiplying with a test function and integrating over the domain. This approach relaxes the differentiability requirements of the solution, allowing for piecewise continuous approximations. The weak form is typically expressed as a bilinear equation involving a bilinear form "
            },
            {
              "type": "inline_math",
              "expression": "B(u, v) = \\langle f, v \\rangle \\quad \\text{for all } v \\in V"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " "
            },
            {
              "type": "citation_occurrence",
              "occurrence_id": "bb0286bc-7083-5fbc-a5e0-72aa6cbfb45b",
              "source_id": "wiki_babu_ka_lax_milgram_theorem"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": ". The coercivity of the bilinear form is a critical property that ensures the existence and uniqueness of the weak solution, guaranteeing that the form is bounded below by a positive multiple of the norm squared of the input function, "
            },
            {
              "type": "inline_math",
              "expression": "|B(u, u)| \\geq c\\|u\\|^2 \\quad \\text{for some } c > 0"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " "
            },
            {
              "type": "citation_occurrence",
              "occurrence_id": "b0dafe50-289d-5c5f-8882-1f73b7615ccd",
              "source_id": "wiki_babu_ka_lax_milgram_theorem"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": ".\n\nThe mathematical foundations of the finite element method rely on the properties of bilinear forms, particularly coercivity and continuity. Coercivity of a bilinear form ensures the existence and uniqueness of the weak solution, guaranteeing that the form is bounded below by a positive multiple of the norm squared of the input function "
            },
            {
              "type": "citation_occurrence",
              "occurrence_id": "412bb1a8-aac9-53a3-948c-8715b540e77f",
              "source_id": "wiki_babu_ka_lax_milgram_theorem"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": ". This property is critical for the stability of numerical methods and is a key condition in the Babuška–Lax–Milgram theorem. The continuity of a bilinear form, defined as "
            },
            {
              "type": "inline_math",
              "expression": "|a(v, w)| \\leq \\gamma \\|v\\| \\|w\\|"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " "
            },
            {
              "type": "citation_occurrence",
              "occurrence_id": "9ab545bf-d1ad-503c-97c2-ce0240aa26ec",
              "source_id": "wiki_c_a_s_lemma"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": ", ensures that the bilinear form does not grow unbounded with respect to the input functions. These properties are essential for the finite element method, as they enable the derivation of error estimates and convergence conditions for the numerical solution."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
